  };
}







Limits of a station series
A station is a real measurement, but not a perfect one: the instruments, the shelter and the surroundings of the airport changed over the decades, and the definition of the daily extremes was modified over time, so part of the long-term drift can come from these changes rather than from the climate. Météo-France applies a climatological quality control, but the series is not homogenised in this visualisation. Years with fewer than 330 days of data are left out, which leaves visible gaps in the vertical axis.

Technical details
The processing is roughly divided into six steps:

choosing a place and downloading its ERA5 daily series
computing the normal temperature of each calendar day over the reference period
subtracting it from every day to get the anomalies
counting the anomalies per year
applying a Gaussian blur to smooth out the graph
offsetting the curve of each year to produce the Joy Division effect


Choosing the place
The place is kept in a mutable value. Three independent cells update it: picking a match in the geocoding results, clicking Load coordinates, or clicking Use my location. Whichever happened last wins. The geocoding comes from the Open-Meteo geocoding API (GeoNames data).

